# NFR-PORT-050 requires line endings to be handled so that files written on one platform
# are identical on another. Normalise everything to LF in the repository; Git may still
# check out CRLF locally where the platform expects it.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Golden-reference audio, models and impulse responses are binary and must never be
# transformed. Corrupting a test fixture by line-ending translation would produce test
# failures that look like DSP bugs.
*.wav  binary
*.aiff binary
*.flac binary
*.nam  binary
*.bin  binary

# M5, docs/02-architecture.md D-2.5's "Consequence (added M5)" note: namir-state's checked-in
# corpus (crates/namir-state/tests/corpus/) proves NFR-PORT-050's byte-level portability
# invariants (no \r, no BOM, canonical float formatting) by asserting on the writer's raw
# output bytes. `* text=auto eol=lf` above would silently normalise a serialiser regression
# that started emitting CRLF back to LF on commit, so the very corpus meant to catch that
# regression would pass against a broken writer. Preset/state documents are plain JSON text,
# but they must be treated as opaque bytes for exactly this reason.
*.namirpreset binary
